{
    "title": "Consumer Leasing (Regulation M)",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 15, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-15/pdf/2025-22813.pdf",
    "summary": "The federal government just announced two notable updates: agencies can offer larger, better-structured hiring and relocation bonuses for hard\u2011to\u2011fill jobs, and the dollar limit that determines when big\u2011ticket consumer leases and loans are covered by federal protections will rise slightly on January 1, 2026.\n\nOn hiring, the Office of Personnel Management updated rules so agencies can, when there\u2019s a clearly defined critical need, waive standard caps and offer recruitment or relocation incentives up to 50% of an employee\u2019s annual basic pay per year of a service agreement, with a hard cap of 100% of annual pay over the full period. These incentives must be justified in writing, approved by designated officials (with extra oversight for waivers), and tied to a written service agreement that can last up to four years.\n\nOn consumer finance, the Federal Reserve and the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau raised the exemption threshold for very large consumer leases and loans from $71,900 to $73,400, reflecting inflation. Transactions above that amount are generally exempt from certain disclosure rules, while mortgages and private student loans remain covered no matter the size. Because the threshold went up, a few more high\u2011dollar deals will now fall under standard consumer protections.\n\nWho benefits? Federal agencies gain flexibility to compete for scarce talent tied to urgent missions, which can help them deliver on new laws, national emergencies, and key initiatives. Candidates and employees in critical roles may see stronger upfront incentives and clearer terms. Consumers also benefit modestly from the finance change, since more big purchases will now come with standard disclosures and protections due to the higher cutoff keeping pace with inflation.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Agency budgets could face higher near\u2011term costs, and uneven use of incentives may create perceptions of inequity if not managed carefully. On the private\u2011sector side, lenders and lessors handling deals just under the new threshold may face slightly more compliance work, while those above it see no change. Overall, these are targeted adjustments: one aimed at strengthening public sector hiring for mission\u2011critical needs, and the other a routine inflation update to keep consumer protections aligned with today\u2019s prices.",
